--TEST--
`Core\IO\File::lock` is one lock whatever `FileMode` opened the handle, and asks for no capability of its own — spec § 14, `rule:security/capability-check-at-the-door`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// A lock changes nothing about the file's contents, so a handle opened `Read`
// may take one — and the capability question was answered at `open` either
// way, which `registry::CAPABILITIES` states as a `None` row for every member
// of this class rather than leaving it to be inferred.
Core\IO\File $seed = Core\IO::open("mode.txt", Core\IO\FileMode::Write);
$seed->write("body");
$seed->close();

Core\IO\File $reader = Core\IO::open("mode.txt", Core\IO\FileMode::Read);
$reader->lock();
echo "a read handle holds it", "\n";

// And because it is the same lock, the mode does not decide who wins: a handle
// opened for writing is refused by one opened for reading. What the mode
// decides is what a handle may do to the file, which is a different question
// from who is holding it.
Core\IO\File $writer = Core\IO::open("mode.txt", Core\IO\FileMode::ReadWrite);
try {
    $writer->lock();
    echo "the writer won", "\n";
} catch (IOError $busy) {
    echo "refused: the reader holds it", "\n";
}

$reader->close();
$writer->lock();
echo "and then the writer holds it", "\n";
$writer->close();
--FILE nvs.toml--
[capabilities.fs]
read = ["."]
write = ["."]
--EXPECT--
a read handle holds it
refused: the reader holds it
and then the writer holds it
